      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-24x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-24{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{5{x}_{0}^{4}-12{x}_{0}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-24{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{5(2)^{4}-12(2)^{2}+1}{20(2)^{3}-24(2)} \\\\  & =1.705\\,357…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{5{x}_{1}^{4}-12{x}_{1}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-24{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.705\\,357…)-\\frac{5(1.705\\,357…)^{4}-12(1.705\\,357…)^{2}+1}{20(1.705\\,357…)^{3}-24(1.705\\,357…)} \\\\  & ≈1.561,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
